{"name":"Moss Rose","hex":"#8f6d6b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moss rose","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rose plant having mossy growths on its sepals due to a genetic mutation first seen in centifolia roses.","The flower of such a rose."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moss_rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moss Rose","blocks":[{"text":"Moss Rose, known as the Leasing.com Stadium for sponsorship reasons, is a football stadium in Macclesfield, Cheshire, England, which is the home ground of Macclesfield F.C., and the former home of Macclesfield Town F.C., a club wound up in September 2020. The stadium, 1 mile (1.6 km) south of the town centre, is on the west side of the A523 London Road. The capacity is currently 5,350. It was built in 1891, making it one of the oldest grounds in England."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Silk_FM_Main_Stand.png/330px-Silk_FM_Main_Stand.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss_Rose","wikidata":"Q1763922"},{"title":"Moss Rose (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Moss Rose is a 1947 American mystery film directed by Gregory Ratoff and starring Peggy Cummins, Victor Mature and Ethel Barrymore. It is an adaptation of the 1934 novel Moss Rose by Marjorie Bowen based on a real-life Victorian murder case."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/10/Moss_rose_poster_small.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss_Rose_(film)","wikidata":"Q2084202"},{"title":"Portulaca grandiflora","blocks":[{"text":"Portulaca grandiflora is a succulent flowering plant in the purslane family Portulacaceae, native to southern Brazil, Argentina, and Uruguay and often cultivated in gardens. It has many common names, including rose moss, eleven o'clock, Mexican rose, moss rose, sun rose, rose Jepun, table rose, rock rose, and moss-rose purslane. Despite these names and the superficial resemblance of some cultivars' flowers to roses, it is not a true rose, nor even a part of the rose family or rosid group; rather, it is much more closely related to carnations and cacti."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/PortulacaGrandiflora.jpg/330px-PortulacaGrandiflora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portulaca_grandiflora","wikidata":"Q159547"},{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss","wikidata":"Q25347"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/%E6%9D%BE%E8%91%89%E7%89%A1%E4%B8%B9_20190403191249.jpg/500px-%E6%9D%BE%E8%91%89%E7%89%A1%E4%B8%B9_20190403191249.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/%E6%9D%BE%E8%91%89%E7%89%A1%E4%B8%B9_20190403191249.jpg/500px-%E6%9D%BE%E8%91%89%E7%89%A1%E4%B8%B9_20190403191249.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portulaca Grandiflora","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E6%9D%BE%E8%91%89%E7%89%A1%E4%B8%B9_20190403191249.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Portulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg/120px-Portulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Portulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg/250px-Portulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Portulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg/120px-Portulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortulaca_in_Kadavoor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Portulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg/120px-Portulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Portulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg/250px-Portulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Portulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg/120px-Portulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortulaca_grandiflora_at_Kadavoor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Portulaca.j